    "lawCode": "Amd §§120, 133 & 134, Ec Dev L; amd §311, Exec L; amd §163, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Economic Development Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1590\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the economic development law, in relation to\nmembership of the small-business advisory board and the minority and\nwomen-owned business advisory board; and to amend the economic\ndevelopment law, the executive law, and the state finance law, in\nrelation to the powers and duties of the division for small-business\nand the division of minority and women's business development\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nTo improve the outreach efforts of the Department of Economic\nDevelopment's Divisions for Small Business and Minority and Women's\nBusiness Development.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSections 1 and 2 add individuals representing banking, community\ndevelopment financial, insurance or surety bonding institutions to\nboth the Minority and Women-Owned Business Enterprise Advisory Board\nand the Small Business Advisory Board. Section 3 removes an outdated\nreference to a repealed section of law and requires the Division for\nSmall Business' information and outreach program for potential state\ncontractors to include information about surety bonding requirements.\nSections 4and 5 expand the powers and duties of the Division for Small\nBusiness, and the Division of Minority and Women's Business\nDevelopment, in providing and facilitating access to programs that\nprovide business development assistance. Section 6 requires the Office\nfor General Services to include information about surety bonding\nrequirements in their annual regional training programs for minority\nand women-owned businesses.\n\n EFFECTS OF PRESENT LAW WHICH THIS BILL WOULD ALTER :\nIncreases membership of advisory boards; specifies inclusion of\ninformation on surety bonding in training programs for potential state\ncontractors; augments powers and duties of Division for Small Business\nand Division of Minority and Women's Business Development.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nIn testimony at Assembly hearings and in published reports, small and\nminority and women-owned businesses have repeatedly expressed the need\nfor better access to working capital, insurance and surety bonding.\nAdding representatives from the financial community to the Small\nBusiness and Minority and Women-Owned Business Advisory Boards will\nfacilitate communication between these two sectors. This legislation\nwill also augment the role of the OED Divisions for Small Business and\nMinority and Women's Business Development as gateways for access to\nthe complex and confusing network of state, federal and local programs\nthat are potential sources of business development assistance,\nincluding financing resources such as loans, loan guarantees, and\nsurety bond guarantees. To conserve scarce state resources, it asks\nOED to seek to maximize the utilization of federal resources available\nto small, minority and women~owned businesses.  Finally, to address\none of the barriers to increased participation by small and minority\nand women-owned businesses in state contracts, this legislation\nensures that the state's outreach programs for small and minority and\nwomen-owned potential state contractors include information on access\nto surety bonding.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008: A.2329-A/S.5202-A\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately, provided that the required appointments pursuant to\nsections 1 and 2 of the Act shall be made by April 1, 2010.\n",
